<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Scramble for Africa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Scramble for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> was the rapid partition and colonisation of most of the African continent by European powers in the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries. It was given diplomatic form by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-conference">Berlin Conference</a> of 1884–1885. It is discussed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a> profiles and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> profile, which describes the British conquest that ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>'s independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/scramble-for-africa.jpg" alt="Scramble for Africa" width="320" height="298" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeff Israel (ZyMOS) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:African-civilizations-map-pre-colonial.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
